  "dom_id": "step-q139183",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14224770/assets/179894a17a2aadf5ba9f463ae3faf2e5836f7b268066f8f56de0f3cf3ff2d8fa.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ and the $x$-axis, shown above. What integral gives the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(2-x) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x=2.$ \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
